3. Thread Oversubscription Fix
The largest overall improvement came from limiting the worker threads created by numerical libraries. Before this change, multiple ROS 2 processes could each create their own OpenMP or BLAS thread pools, causing CPU contention, context switching, extra per-thread memory, and unstable callback scheduling on the six-core Jetson.
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1
export OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1
export MKL_NUM_THREADS=1
export NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS=1
These exports were placed inside the Docker shell that launches the ROS 2 stack, ensuring every node started by ros2 launch inherited the limits.
docker exec -it my_robotics_env /bin/bash -c "
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1;
export OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1;
export MKL_NUM_THREADS=1;
export NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS=1;
source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash;
source /workspace/install/setup.bash;
exec ros2 launch robot_bringup params_my_robot.launch.py
"

export MKL_NUM_THREADS=1;
export NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS=1;
source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash;
source /workspace/install/setup.bash;
exec ros2 launch robot_bringup params_my_robot.launch.py
"
Observed effects included lower RAM usage, much more stable FPS, reduced tracker CPU, and more predictable scheduling across the complete robot stack.
4. Camera Heartbeat Optimization
The watchdog originally subscribed directly to /camera/image_raw. That forced it to receive and deserialize 640x480 color images at camera rate even though it only needed to know whether the camera was alive.
